rotocol on storage, encryption, and cost management is essential for the system to be viable over its lifetime. If we can't afford to store the logs, we aren't compliant; if we aren't compliant, the product can't be deployed.\n\n    *   Does it help speed/quality of prototyping? Indirectly, yes, by ensuring the underlying infrastructure is sound and funded.\n    *   Does it address technical viability? Yes, storage and encryption are core technical concerns for \"lifetime\" logging.\n    *   Is it necessary? Without a protocol, the implementation will be haphazard, likely leading to expensive migrations or security breaches (GDPR/DPO issues) that would halt development later.\n\n    *   Rationale: A standardized protocol for log storage and cost management is critical to prevent technical debt and budget erosion, ensuring that lifetime logging requirements don't compromise R&D capacity. It provides the necessary infrastructure constraints within which we can prototype viable, compliant systems.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (around 85-90). It's a practical necessity for any long-term project.",
