gests creating a *permanent department/team* just for log analytics. For an R&D institution where resources are scarce and focused on scientific ambition, this feels like \"compliance bloat.\"\n    *   We can achieve the regulatory goal (traceability and monitoring) through automated tooling integrated into the DevOps/MLOps pipeline without needing a dedicated human analyst team solely for compliance reporting.\n    *   However, from a risk-mitigation perspective, having some form of this is necessary to avoid massive fines under the AI Act. But \"permanent function\" + \"dedicated team\" = very high resource cost.\n\n    *   Is it necessary? Yes, we need monitoring.\n    *   Is *this specific implementation* (a dedicated analyst team/permanent function) efficient? No. It's an organizational overhead that competes with R&D.\n    *   Decision: Reject. I would prefer a technical solution integrated into existing workflows rather than a separate administrative/analyst silo.\n\n    *Self-Correction during drafting:* Wait, if this is \"high-risk AI,\" the penalties for non-compliance are astronomical (similar to GDPR). Does that justify the cost? As Lab Director, my job is efficiency and ambition. I can't just throw people at a problem; I need an efficient use of resources. A dedicated team for *log analytics* specifically seems like an inefficient way to handle compliance when these should be automated telemetry alerts managed by the system owners.\n\n    *   Rationale: Creating a permanent, dedicated analyst function introduces significant operational overhead and diverts scarce headcount away from core scientific R&D. Compliance monitoring should be integrated into existing MLOps pipelines via automation rather than as a standalone bureaucratic layer.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 30 (The *goal* is right, but the *method*—a dedicated team/function—is resource-inefficient).",
