itutional Survival/Reputation:* A publishable architectural contribution that solves a regulatory requirement natively would be a massive reputational win. It positions the lab as a leader in \"Compliant AI.\"\n    *   *Practicality vs. Compliance:* Does Art 12 *require* layer-level transparency? No. It requires logging of events (start/end time, DB references, inputs). Most of what's requested in Art 12(3) happens at the *application* level (who used it and when), not the *neural layer* level.\n    *   *Critical Flaw:* The proposal focuses on \"intermediate steps\" and \"layer-level\" provenance. Article 12 is about *event logging* (e.g., \"the system was started at 10:00 AM by User X\"). Embedding a timestamp or a user ID into a neural layer's attention mechanism is technically overkill and likely irrelevant to the legal requirements of Art 12, which are focused on operational traceability rather than mathematical interpretability.\n    *   *Wait, reconsider:* While Art 12(3) focuses on usage logs, Recital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed... algorithms, data, training, testing.\" Native provenance *could* assist with the broader spirit of transparency and traceability.\n    *   *However,* as a Compliance Officer concerned with *institutional survival*, I worry about spending scarce resources on a \"research problem\" for something that can be solved by standard software engineering (logging wrappers). If the goal is to satisfy Art 12, this is an over-engineered solution that risks failure and doesn't actually solve the basic requirements (like recording the natural person who verified the result—which happens *outside* the model).\n\n    *   The proposal conflates \"model interpretability\" with \"regulatory record-keeping.\"\n    *   Article 12 requires logs of *events* (use cases, timestamps, database matches), which are system-level events, not tensor-level operations.\n    *   Spending resources on \"trace tokens\" in neural modules is a research project for interpretability, but it does not directly satisfy the operational logging requirements of Article 12 more effectively than a standard log file would.\n    *   Therefore, from a *Compliance/Survival* perspective, this is an inefficient use of resources that provides \"academic prestige\" but doesn't secure regulatory compliance any better than existing engineering tools.\n\n    *Wait*, if the institution is an R&D lab, \"market reputation\" includes being seen as a pioneer in AI safety/compliance. But \"institutional survival\" means not wasting money on things that don't work or aren't needed.\n    Let's look at Art 12(3) again:\n    - (a) period of use. (System level)\n    - (b) reference database. (System/Data level)\n    - (c) input data match. (Input/Output level)\n    - (d) identification of natural persons. (User level)\n\n    None of these are \"layer-level\" events. The proposal is trying to solve a different problem (interpretability/provenance) and labeling it as an Art 12 solution. As Compliance Officer, I cannot approve a project that fundamentally misunderstands the regulatory requirement in favor of academic interest.",
