ove quality.\n        *   *Cons:* Adds a gate at the start (\"No high-risk project proceeds without...\"). This could potentially slow down the *initiation* of prototyping if the review process is bureaucratic or slow.\n    *   *Overall Alignment with Role:* As a Dev Engineer, I care about not wasting my team's time on rework and having clear technical requirements. While it adds an upfront step, the risk of retroactive compliance engineering in AI systems (which often involves changing data pipelines and state management) is a significant threat to velocity and quality.\n\n    *   Does this help achieve Article 12? Yes, by ensuring logging obligations are identified and specified early.\n    *   Is it worth scarce resources? It's primarily a process change involving legal and project leads. The \"implementation resource\" here is the time spent on the checklist and review. Given the cost of failure (legal non-compliance or massive rework), this is an efficient use of resources.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Establishing clear logging requirements at the start prevents expensive architectural rework later in the development cycle, which preserves long-term prototyping speed and technical quality.\n    *   *Decision:* approve.\n    *   *Promise:* High (around 85-90), because \"shifting left\" on compliance is an engineering best practice.",
