ecific domain of concern:* \"Technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n        *   Is this solution technically viable? Yes.\n        *   Does it impact the speed/quality of prototyping? It adds an audit layer. If I'm building a prototype, does a quarterly audit help me? Not really. But if we are moving toward production (which high-risk systems must), ensuring the logs actually work is critical to prevent massive redesigns later when a regulator finds the logs are empty or corrupted.\n    *   *Critical Analysis:* The proposal isn't about *implementing* the logging (the technical part) but *auditing* it. As an engineer, I usually hate \"compliance overhead,\" but this is a very lightweight check (50 samples per quarter). It doesn't require me to build complex automation beyond perhaps some query tools for the auditors.\n\n    *   The proposal asks for *compliance staff* to do the work, not the engineering team.\n    *   It validates that the technical implementation of Article 12(3) is actually functioning in production.\n    *   From a viability standpoint: It's trivial to implement (just provide access to logs).\n    *   From a prototyping/quality standpoint: It ensures a \"closed loop\" where we know if our logging logic is failing before the regulator tells us.\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal describes a low-overhead verification process that validates technical implementation without requiring complex new engineering features or slowing down development cycles. It ensures that the logging requirements are actually being met in practice, reducing the risk of discovering critical telemetry gaps late in the lifecycle.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (High because it's low cost/low effort for engineers but provides high confidence that the technical requirements were correctly implemented).\n\n    *   Did I use my persona? Yes (Dev Engineer).\n    *   Did I follow the format? Rationale -> Decision -> Promise.\n    * 