h to compliance.\n        *   Avoids \"technical debt\" where developers forget to add logging before release.\n        *   Maintains R&D velocity (no overhead of persistent storage for throwaway tests).\n        *   The \"promotion checklist\" integration adds a layer of governance.\n    *   *Cons/Risks:*\n        *   Dependency on the feature-flag service and promotion checklist. If those fail, a system could hit market in \"ephemeral mode.\"\n        *   Does it cover *all* Article 12 requirements (specifically 12(3) for Annex III)? The proposal claims it is \"Article 12(3)-conformant,\" but as Compliance Officer, I need to trust the implementation. However, the *architectural approach* is sound.\n\n    *   The proposal explicitly separates \"development status\" from \"market/staging.\"\n    *   It provides a technical mechanism to ensure compliance at the critical juncture (graduation).\n    *   It minimizes the friction that often leads developers to bypass compliance measures.\n    *   Verdict: Approve. It aligns with the goal of ensuring compliance without stifling R&D, provided the \"promotion checklist\" is strictly enforced.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal ensures a rigorous transition from prototyping to production-ready compliance, mitigating the risk of deploying high-risk systems without required logging. By integrating the switch into the promotion checklist and using a unified API, it reduces human error in the compliance chain.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (e.g., 90). It's an elegant solution to a common friction point between R&D speed and regulatory requirements.",
