d start/end times.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* While theoretically elegant and good for reputation, this is a research project rather than a direct implementation of required logging controls; regulatory compliance requires adherence to legal standards, not mathematical minima which may be viewed as insufficient by auditors.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject (because it's framed as a \"research programme\" rather than an \"implementation,\" and the goal of finding the *minimum* is risky for compliance). Actually, let me reconsider. If I reject everything that is research, I might miss out on the \"R&D\" part of the institution. But as Compliance Officer, my priority is survival/compliance. Researching how to do the *minimum* feels like a liability when facing EU regulators.\n\n    *   *Alternative View:* Maybe it's an \"approve\"? It positions us as experts. No, the phrase \"minimum set\" is a red flag for a Compliance Officer. We want a \"sufficient and defensible set.\"\n    *   Let's look at the prompt again: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it... not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" This research program *is* exploration.\n\n    *   Rationale: The focus on finding \"minimum\" logging requirements through theoretical bounds poses a regulatory risk, as compliance is determined by legal adequacy and auditability rather than information-theoretic efficiency. Additionally, this is framed as a research programme rather than a concrete implementation of the mandatory record-keeping requirements.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 30 (It has some reputational value, but it's not a compliance tool).\n\n    Wait, if I am an R&D institution, maybe \"researching\" is what we do? But the prompt asks me to screen from my *professional vantage point* as Compliance Officer. A Compliance Officer wants certainty and safety, not an optimization problem that might leave gaps.\n\n    *Self-Correction: