een developed (provenance) and how they perform (logs) are both essential for traceability.\n    *   Risk assessment: Lack of proper documentation during the development phase can lead to failure in compliance audits under the AI Act. Implementing an automated, append-only ledger reduces the risk of \"documentation gaps\" or retrospective fabrication of records, which is a major regulatory risk.\n    *   Market reputation/Institutional survival: Openly publishing schemas as benchmarks positions the lab as a leader in responsible AI and transparency, enhancing market reputation.\n\n    *   The proposal addresses the *technical documentation* requirement mentioned in Recital 71 (\"information which is necessary to assess the compliance... algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\").\n    *   While it doesn't implement the \"automatic recording of events\" (runtime logs) required by Article 12(1), it provides the foundational traceability for the development lifecycle mandated by Recital 71.\n    *   The use of an append-only ledger ensures integrity, which is critical for regulatory audits.\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal directly implements the documentation requirements of Recital 71 regarding training and validation processes via a machine-readable provenance graph, reducing audit risk and enhancing institutional reputation through transparency.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (around 85-90). It's highly practical since it builds on existing tools (DVC, W&B) but transforms them into a compliance asset.",
