oals?* It introduces bureaucracy (\"gate in our software development lifecycle,\" \"binding technical standard\"). However, this is a regulatory requirement (Article 12). The act isn't optional.\n\n    *   The proposal focuses on *implementation* and *compliance*. As a researcher, I generally view \"gates\" and \"mandatory specs\" as overhead unless they serve the research goals.\n    *   However, Article 12 is a hard requirement for high-risk systems. Failing this means no product/deployment in the EU.\n    *   From a scientific impact perspective, having structured, consistent logs across all high-risk systems (as proposed via \"log schemas\") creates a rich data source for analyzing AI behavior and reliability—this *does* have intellectual merit. It turns compliance into a research asset.\n    *   The proposal's primary focus is risk mitigation (\"protects the institution from post-market enforcement\"), which isn't my core domain, but ensuring the lab can keep operating is fundamental.\n\n    *   Rationale: Standardizing logging architecture not only ensures regulatory compliance—essential for continued operation in the EU—but also provides a consistent data framework for analyzing system behavior and failure modes across different projects, enhancing our scientific understanding of AI reliability.\n    *   Decision: approve (Because it's necessary for operation and offers telemetry benefits).\n    *   Promise: 85 (Strong because it solves a hard requirement and adds research value, though it is more \"engineering\" than \"pure research\").",
